  <p class="lead-text">
    Many Mississippi homeowners don't discover how their wind and hail deductible works until a storm damages their roof. By then, it's too late to change it. Here's how these deductibles work and how to choose the right one.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What Is a Separate Wind/Hail Deductible?</h2>
  <p>
    Most homeowners policies have an "all other perils" (AOP) deductible, usually a flat dollar amount like $1,000 or $2,500, that applies to claims like fire, theft, or a burst pipe.
  </p>
  <p>
    In storm-prone states like Mississippi, many policies also have a <strong>separate deductible for wind and hail damage</strong>. It's often a <strong>percentage of your dwelling coverage limit</strong> (Coverage A), rather than a flat dollar amount.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Percentage Deductibles in Real Dollars</h2>
  <p>
    The key thing to understand: the percentage is based on how much your <strong>home is insured for</strong>, not the size of the claim.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Dwelling Coverage</th><th>1% Deductible</th><th>2% Deductible</th><th>5% Deductible</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>$200,000</td><td>$2,000</td><td>$4,000</td><td>$10,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$250,000</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$12,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$3,500</td><td>$7,000</td><td>$17,500</td></tr>
      <tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$25,000</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout-box">
    <h3>⚠️ Example</h3>
    <p>Your home is insured for $300,000 with a 2% wind/hail deductible. A hailstorm causes $9,000 in roof damage. You pay the first $6,000 and your policy pays $3,000. With a $1,000 flat deductible, the policy would have paid $8,000.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>3. Tornadoes, Thunderstorms & Hurricanes</h2>
  <p>
    North Mississippi sees frequent severe thunderstorms, hail, straight-line winds, and tornadoes, especially in spring. Damage from these storms is generally treated as <strong>wind or hail damage</strong>, so your wind/hail deductible usually applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some policies, particularly closer to the Gulf Coast, also include a separate <strong>named storm or hurricane deductible</strong> that applies only when a storm has been named by the National Weather Service. Your declarations page will spell out which deductibles apply to your home.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Watch Your Roof Coverage</h2>
  <p>
    Some carriers pay for older roofs on an <strong>actual cash value</strong> basis (replacement cost minus depreciation) instead of full replacement cost, or use a roof payment schedule based on age. Combined with a percentage deductible, that can leave a much larger gap than homeowners expect on an older roof.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Should You Choose a Higher or Lower Deductible?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Higher deductible:</strong> Lower premium, but more out of pocket after a storm. Best if you have a solid emergency fund.</li>
    <li><strong>Lower or flat deductible:</strong> Higher premium, but more predictable costs when storms hit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A good rule of thumb: <strong>don't choose a deductible larger than what you could comfortably pay tomorrow</strong>. Availability of lower deductibles varies by carrier and location, which is why comparing multiple companies matters.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Ways to Lower Your Storm Costs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Impact-resistant roofing:</strong> Many carriers offer discounts for Class 4 impact-resistant shingles.</li>
    <li><strong>FORTIFIED roofs:</strong> Roofs built to the IBHS FORTIFIED standard may qualify for discounts with many carriers. Ask us before you re-roof.</li>
    <li><strong>Bundle home and auto:</strong> Multi-policy discounts can offset the cost of a lower deductible.</li>
    <li><strong>Shop independently:</strong> Deductible options and pricing vary widely between carriers.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3>✅ After a Storm</h3>
    <p>Take photos, make temporary repairs to prevent further damage (like tarping a roof), keep your receipts, and call us before signing anything with a storm-chasing contractor. We'll help you understand your deductible and guide the claim.</p>
  </div>
